Title: ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION fails intermittently on a broken pipe
Status: CLOSED 2.2.3 -- root cause found + fixed 2026-09-10
Found: 2026-09-06, in passing, running the suite for an unrelated change

SYMPTOM 1 (2026-09-06)

    ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION in CLAUTOLISP-TOOL-SUITE:
      Unexpected Error: #<SB-INT:BROKEN-PIPE ...>
      Couldn't write to #<SB-SYS:FD-STREAM for
        "socket 127.0.0.1:44119, peer: 127.0.0.1:46746">:
        Broken pipe.

One failure out of 33 suites. The IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWING run of the same
suite, same image, same tree, was green: 33 suites, 0 failures. Nothing
between the two runs changed.

WHY IT IS WORTH A TICKET RATHER THAN A SHRUG

A test that fails once in a while is worse than one that fails always.
It teaches people to re-run rather than to look, and the day it fails
for a REAL reason it will be re-run too. This project has already spent
days on the opposite failure -- a silence read as evidence -- and a
flake is the same coin: an outcome nobody trusts enough to act on.

It also lands in CI, where a red pipeline on a merge request costs a
maintainer's attention before anyone knows it was noise.

NOT INTRODUCED BY THE COMPILER BRANCH: it appeared while running the
suite for a documentation change, on a tree whose only code delta was
one new compiler test, and the listener is master's work. Recorded here
rather than in that branch's issues because it belongs to the debugger
UI, not the compiler.

SYMPTOM 2 (2026-09-09, GitLab job 16389774638, test:clautolisp:windows)

Same test, a DIFFERENT symptom -- not a write failing on a broken pipe,
but the whole ORIGINAL AutoLISP error escaping uncaught:

    ALDB-LISTENER-DRIVES-A-LIVE-SOCKET-SESSION in CLAUTOLISP-TOOL-SUITE []:
         Unexpected Error: #<CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-RUNTIME:AUTOLISP-RUNTIME-ERROR {1104DCD723}>
    Undefined AutoLISP function ALDB-NO-SUCH-FUNCTION..

"Did 1 check" (not the normal 6): the escape happens before the test body's
first `is`, i.e. inside the `(let ((outcome (... call-with-session ...))))`
binding itself, so FiveAM records the whole test as one failed check rather
than any specific assertion.

ROOT CAUSE (found 2026-09-10, reproduced locally on macOS -- no CAD, no
Windows runner needed, confirming the "it needs no CAD and no runner" note
below)

Reproduced by running the raw test body in a tight loop (a few hundred to a
few thousand iterations); failure rate ~0.3-1%, matching "one in 33 suites"
and "3 in 300" from earlier sampling. Traced with `*on-error*`/`*on-failure*`
bound to `:debug` (so FiveAM's own handler declines and the condition
reaches an outer HANDLER-BIND while the stack is still live) plus temporary
instrumentation on DEBUG-HANDLE-ERROR / APPLY-ERROR-DIRECTIVE /
%ALDB-DRIVE-CONNECTED.

On a failing run:

    [trace] DEBUG-HANDLE-ERROR called, reason=UNHANDLED-ERROR break-on-error=T
    [trace] server: aldb-wait-for-delegate returned after 0.000s
    [trace] APPLY-ERROR-DIRECTIVE got :CONTINUE
    [trace] client wire = ""
    [trace] client: 15s inactivity timeout, elapsed=0.005s

*BREAK-ON-ERROR* is T and the debugger DOES engage (rules out an earlier
"maybe *break-on-error* leaked to NIL" hypothesis -- it does not; CL's
dynamic LET/UNWIND-PROTECT scoping around it holds exactly as designed).
The server side (aldb-wait-for-delegate accepting the already-queued
connection) is fine, near-instant every time.

The bug is in the TEST'S OWN client driver, %ALDB-DRIVE-CONNECTED:

    (unless (usocket:wait-for-input sock :timeout 15 :ready-only t)
      (return))                               ; safety: no data for 15s

USOCKET:WAIT-FOR-INPUT can return NIL ("not ready") well before its
requested timeout elapses -- observed as a 15-SECOND wait returning FALSE
in under 5 MILLISECONDS. Most likely an interrupted underlying
select/poll/kqueue call (plausibly provoked by the OTHER thread this test
spawns doing its own socket syscalls concurrently) that some layer under
USOCKET mishandles as a genuine timeout instead of retrying it. The
client's ORIGINAL code treated any single NIL as "genuinely 15s of
silence" and gave up immediately, closing its socket having read NOTHING
-- not even the first (:attached ...) message, which the server had not
even had a chance to write yet.

That is what produces BOTH observed symptoms:
  - Symptom 2 (this run): the server, moments later, tries to READ the
    client's (:abort) reply and gets immediate EOF (the peer already
    closed) -- EMACS-UI's UI-AWAIT-COMMAND treats EOF as "the client went
    away", returning :CONTINUE, which is the documented "decline -> let it
    propagate to the user's *error*" resolution (autolisp-debug/source/
    error.lisp). With no *error* handler installed (the test deliberately
    uses a bare context), the original AUTOLISP-RUNTIME-ERROR just
    continues propagating, unhandled, exactly as observed.
  - Symptom 1 (the original report): a variant of the same race -- the
    server writes into (or closes) a socket the client already abandoned,
    surfacing as SB-INT:BROKEN-PIPE on a write instead.

Confirms the original "WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE" guess below (a race between the
client and the server, widening under load) was on the right track, but
narrows it to a specific, fixable culprit: WAIT-FOR-INPUT's :TIMEOUT is not
safe to trust as a single call in this client -- it must be retried against
a real wall-clock deadline, the same way the PRODUCTION accept loop
(aldb-wait-for-delegate, tools/clautolisp/source/main.lisp) already does
(it re-polls every 1s in a genuine LOOP, so a single spurious NIL there
costs nothing) -- the test's one-shot 15s wait was the only place trusting
a single call.

FIX (clautolisp 2.2.3)

tools/clautolisp/tests/aldb-socket-tests.lisp: added
%ALDB-WAIT-FOR-INPUT-UNTIL, which retries USOCKET:WAIT-FOR-INPUT against a
GET-INTERNAL-REAL-TIME deadline (computed once, 15s out) instead of trusting
one call's :TIMEOUT; %ALDB-DRIVE-CONNECTED now calls it instead of a bare
WAIT-FOR-INPUT. Verified with 5000 successive runs of the raw test body
locally (0 failures; the unfixed version had already reproduced at ~0.3-1%
in the same harness) plus one full `asdf:test-system "clautolisp"` pass
(191/191).

WHAT WAS NOT DONE, ON PURPOSE

Production code (aldb-wait-for-delegate) was NOT touched: its accept loop
already retries every 1s regardless of any single WAIT-FOR-INPUT result, so
it was never exposed to this. Nothing here points at a real bug in the
debugger's protocol or its error-decline semantics (:CONTINUE-on-EOF is
correct -- a real Emacs client vanishing mid-session should let the error
propagate rather than hang the host forever); the fix is entirely in the
test's use of WAIT-FOR-INPUT.

OPEN QUESTION, IF THIS RECURS ELSEWHERE

USOCKET:WAIT-FOR-INPUT's early-return behaviour under a concurrent socket
syscall in another thread looks like it could bite ANY caller in this
codebase that treats a single WAIT-FOR-INPUT call as authoritative rather
than retrying against a deadline (upstream-dependency-bug-policy: worth a
grep across the tree, and if usocket/SBCL's own EINTR handling turns out to
be the small dependency bug at the bottom of this, an upstream MR is the
right next step -- not yet done here, since only this one call site was
confirmed affected).
